  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nSurface low pressure is moving across southern MN this morning with \na weak surface boundary crossing through the Northland. Aloft we \nhave a strong mid level trough that is helping to propel this system \neast with the strongest forcing being observed well to our south. \nOur area has been able to see some shower generation along some lingering\noutflow boundaries from yesterday as well as the passing surface\nboundary. But overall coverage is fairly limited and the latest\nhigh res guidance that has come in is favoring a more southerly\ntrack for rain. Largely keeping it across the Brainerd Lakes \nregion and east into portions of NW WI. This forecast package \nhas trimmed the northern extent of PoPs and lowered chances to \n20-40% through the rest of the day. Thunderstorm chances are \nquite low with model soundings showing little instability and \ndeep saturation. Steering flow is rather slow, if any showers do\nmanage to form they could lead to a good soaking for impacted \nareas with PWATs over 1.5 inches. The only storm potential looks\nto be in the afternoon hours if the frontal boundary remains \ndraped across NW WI, but with the extensive cloud cover from the\nslow moving system; instability recovery seems unlikely. With \nthe extensive cloud cover and the surface front inching across \nthe region today, it will be noticeably cooler with highs in the\nmid 70s.\n\nWeekend-Next Week:\n\nQuiet weather is expected through the weekend as a surface high \npressure slides across Ontario and through the Great Lakes. \nTemperatures will be slightly higher than seasonable averages with \nhighs forecasted to be in the 80s. Fortunately dewpoints will stay \nin the 60s keeping the oppressive heat at bay. Diurnal heating \nSaturday afternoon will pair off with a weak disturbance aloft that \ncould generate some showers. The most likely area for this would be \nalong the North Shore as the added orographic from winds off the \nlake may be the boost needed for some activity. For now, this is \nonly a 20-30% chance. Monday starts to warm up with highs \nclimbing into the upper 80s as southerly return flow from the \ndeparting high pressure streams back into the Northland. This \nwarm up will be breif as another system slides in late Monday \nthrough Tuesday bringing more rain and storm chances with \ncooler weather on the backside.",
